%I A397831 #36 Nov 04 2026 12:34:39
%S A397831 2,3,5,7,9,11,13,15,17,21,23,25,28,31,33,37,39,41,44,47,49,55,57,60,
%T A397831 63,65,69,71,73,76,79,81,87,89,92,95,97,101,103,105,108,111,113,121,
%U A397831 123,125,127,129,133,137,141,143,145,148,150,154,156,158,161,164
%N A397831 Indices of ones in A400000.
%C A397831 This sequence is infinite as each record high point in A400000 is followed by a value 1 and A400000 is unbounded.
%C A397831 a(n) is the position of the (n+1)-th record high in A397809. Proof: From the definition of A400000, if the m-th building has height 1, it can see only the preceding building, which is later than any building seen from building k for k < m: a new record. Otherwise (and for m > 1) the m-th building can see over the top of the preceding building to see an earlier building, in which case it can see the earliest building B that the preceding building can see, as B will be at the same or a higher angle of elevation from both viewpoints; hence no new record. - _Peter Munn_ and _Jon Maiga_, Oct 30 2026
%H A397831 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A397831/b397831.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A397831 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A397831/a397831.txt">C++ program</a>
%e A397831 A400000(41) = 1 so 41 belongs to the sequence.
%e A397831 A400000(42) = 2 so 42 does not belong to the sequence.
%t A397831 (* A400000list is defined at A400000 *)
%t A397831 PositionIndex[A400000list[200]][1] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 27 2026 *)
